      "summary": "House Republican legislators held a policy committee hearing in Allegheny County to examine the legal gap created after the Pennsylvania Supreme Court struck down automatic life-without-parole sentences for second-degree murder convictions. Prosecutors testified about the potential consequences of the unresolved sentencing framework, including inconsistent sentences and repeated court proceedings for victims' families. The committee heard testimony in support of Senate Bill 1400, which would establish a minimum 35-year sentence with a maximum of life imprisonment, while Republicans blamed House Democrats for allowing the court-imposed 120-day deadline to pass without a final legislative solution.",
